  entryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Returning home after picking up yarn from the market.
    - Resident returning to the southern apartment block.
    - Visiting a neighbor to help hem some new curtains.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Seeking shelter from the heavy smog in the district.
    - Checking on the status of the local communal kitchen.
    - Seeking refuge from the radioactive dust storms.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - Returning to assigned bunk for the final lockdown.
    - Seeking refuge from the falling black ash.
    - Fulfilling mandatory elderly residency check-in.
  invalidEntryReasons:
    earlyDaysReasons:
    - Going to the theater for a 10:00 PM opera.
    - Picking up a pension check from a bank that closed in 1960.
    - Buying fresh strawberries in the middle of a blizzard.
    midDaysReasons:
    - Returning to a house that was condemned for radiation.
    - Meeting a sister who passed away during the Great Frost.
    - Taking a walk in the park while the chemical sirens are active.
    finalDaysReasons:
    - Going home to plant flowers in the radioactive ash.
    - I need to go buy more silk for a wedding dress.
    - To wait for the sun to come out from behind the smoke.
  entryDialogues:
    lastVerdict: 0
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Hello, dear. You look thin; are they feeding you enough at this booth?
    - Here are my papers. I kept them under my Bible so they wouldn't wrinkle.
    - "It\u2019s a bit chilly today. I hope you\u2019re wearing your thermal vest
      under that uniform."
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - There is a small tear in your sleeve, dear. Bring it here, I have my needle.
    - "The sky looks so bruised lately. I think I\u2019ll go home and make some soup."
    - My hands are shaking a bit more today. The air has a very metallic taste.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - "I\u2019m going home to finish a sweater for my grandson. It\u2019s a very
      bright red."
    - "Don't worry about the sirens, dear. It\u2019s just the world having a little
      tantrum."
    - "Everything is going to be fine once I finish the mending. You\u2019ll see."
  exitDialoguesPassed:
    lastVerdict: 1
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Bless you, dear. Stay warm and don't work too hard.
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - Go home soon. The air isn't good for young lungs like yours.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - "I\u2019ll save a bowl of soup for you. Be safe in the quiet."
  exitDialoguesQuarantined:
    lastVerdict: 2
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - Oh, am I staying here? Well, at least it gives me time to knit.
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - Is the heater working in there? My bones get so cold these days.
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - A nice quiet room. I can finally finish my stitching in peace.
  exitDialoguesKilled:
    lastVerdict: 3
    dialoguesEarlyDays:
    - "Oh... I think... I\u2019ve dropped... my thimble..."
    dialoguesMidDays:
    - "My... threads... they\u2019re all... getting tangled..."
    dialoguesFinalDays:
    - "It\u2019s okay... the world... was getting... too heavy... anyway..."
  voiceAudioClips: []
  questionResponses:
  - question: Where are you coming from?
    earlyDaysAnswer: The bakery. I open early and close late.
    midDaysAnswer: The supply depot. Flour has been hard to find.
    finalDaysAnswer: The bakery. We're still open. Someone has to be.
  - question: Have you been experiencing any strange symptoms lately?
    earlyDaysAnswer: I've had a cough. The flour dust, I always say.
    midDaysAnswer: My throat has been sore for two weeks. It's not getting worse
      though.
    finalDaysAnswer: I feel heavy. Like everything weighs more than it should.
  - question: Who do you live with?
    earlyDaysAnswer: My husband. He helps at the bakery on weekends.
    midDaysAnswer: My husband. He's been unwell. Staying home.
    finalDaysAnswer: My husband. He needs looking after.
  IDNumber: 7802070
